{"id":"n-20261028-jlctan","category":"travel","display_title":"Northern Ireland's Narnian Landscapes","display_title_he":"נופי נרניה בצפון אירלנד","short_summary":"Discover how the striking landscapes of Northern Ireland and the childhood memories of Belfast-born author CS Lewis inspired the magical kingdom of Narnia.","english_level":"C1","words":461,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/travel/2026/oct/27/cs-lewis-narnia-inspiration-northern-ireland-belfast","title":"‘The view from the Mournes was CS Lewis’s idea of Narnia’: how Northern Ireland inspired a magical kingdom","published_at":"2026-10-27T07:00:06.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-27T07:00:06.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-28T07:02:36.579Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["Long before Netflix brought the magical realm back to screens, the breathtaking landscapes of Northern Ireland deeply shaped the imagination of CS Lewis. Born in Dundela, east Belfast, in 1898, the renowned author grew up surrounded by a tough, working-class industrial city that sat right on the edge of expansive fields, rolling hills, and woodland glens. These environments provided the young Lewis and his elder brother Warnie with an extraordinary sense of freedom to explore the countryside on foot, laying the groundwork for the fantastical worlds he would later create.","Today, east Belfast openly embraces its literary heritage. Amidst the working-class streets, paramilitary murals, and complex local history, visitors can find CS Lewis Square in the Holywood Arches neighbourhood. This public space features a walking trail adorned with metal sculptures of beloved characters from The Chronicles of Narnia, including Mr and Mrs Beaver and Mr Tumnus, all dominated by a towering statue of Aslan atop a circular mound. Just a short walk away, St Mark’s church on the Holywood Road houses an exact replica of a brass lion’s head doorknob that once decorated the rectory where Lewis’s grandfather, the Rev Thomas Hamilton, ministered for 13 years, serving as a frequent pilgrimage site for fans.","Further into the city, the large redbrick family home known as Little Lea on Circular Road directly influenced the narrative of the books. According to Lewis's autobiography Surprised By Joy, the sprawling house felt like an entire city to a child, featuring long corridors, empty sunlit rooms, and attics explored in solitude. That very atmosphere was immortalised when characters in The Magician’s Nephew and The Lion, the Witch and the Wardrobe discover a path to another world while exploring a large attic or playing hide and seek.","Beyond the city streets, the broader geography of Northern Ireland echoes throughout the Narnian chronicles. Lewis explicitly told his brother Warnie that the stunning view from the Mourne Mountains above Rostrevor looking down over Carlingford Lough represented his true idea of Narnia. Furthermore, scholars and passionate fans continue to debate which coastal landmarks inspired Cair Paravel, the great castle capital of the fictional realm. Some suggest the precipitous ruins of Dunluce Castle on the north coast, while others point to Downhill Demesne and the iconic Mussenden Temple, which appears to match the description of a great star resting upon the seashore.","As anticipation builds for the upcoming release of Netflix’s new adaptation, Narnia: The Magician’s Nephew, arriving in cinemas in February 2027 and on the platform in April, travellers and literary enthusiasts continue to trace these remarkable routes. Whether standing in CS Lewis Square or gazing across the Mourne Mountains, visitors can easily perceive how the majestic topography of Northern Ireland blossomed into one of the most famous fantasy kingdoms in literary history."],"facts":[{"fact":"CS Lewis was born in Belfast in 1898 and drew inspiration for Narnia from Northern Ireland's landscapes.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"CS Lewis Square in east Belfast features metal sculptures of characters from The Chronicles of Narnia, including a large Aslan.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"St Mark’s church on the Holywood Road has a replica of a brass lion’s head doorknob from the rectory where Lewis's grandfather lived.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Little Lea, the Lewis family home on Circular Road, featured in the opening two Narnia books.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Lewis once told his brother Warnie that the view from the Mournes above Rostrevor looking over Carlingford Lough was his idea of Narnia.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Scholars debate whether Dunluce Castle or Downhill Demesne inspired Cair Paravel, the castle capital of Narnia.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Netflix's upcoming adaptation, Narnia: The Magician’s Nephew, is scheduled for release in early 2027.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"long","lemma":"long","pos":"adj","he":"ארוך","name":""},{"text":"before","lemma":"before","pos":"prep","he":"לפני","name":""},{"text":"brought","lemma":"bring","pos":"v","he":"הביא","name":""},{"text":"magical","lemma":"magical","pos":"adj","he":"קסום","name":""},{"text":"realm","lemma":"realm","pos":"n","he":"ממלכה, תחום","name":""},{"text":"screens","lemma":"screen","pos":"n","he":"מסכים","name":""},{"text":"breathtaking","lemma":"breathtaking","pos":"adj","he":"מהמם, עוצר נשימה","name":""},{"text":"landscapes","lemma":"landscape","pos":"n","he":"נופים","name":""},{"text":"deeply","lemma":"deeply","pos":"adv","he":"עמוקות","name":""},{"text":"shaped","lemma":"shape","pos":"v","he":"עיצב","name":""},{"text":"imagination","lemma":"imagination","pos":"n","he":"דמיון","name":""},{"text":"born","lemma":"bear","pos":"v","he":"נולד","name":""},{"text":"renowned","lemma":"renowned","pos":"adj","he":"ידוע שם, מפורסם","name":""},{"text":"author","lemma":"author","pos":"n","he":"מחבר, סופר","name":""},{"text":"grew up","lemma":"grow up","pos":"v",